---
title: How to Set Default Payment Terms
description: >-
  Choose how many days after the issue date a new invoice falls due, and Taskip
  fills in the due date for you.
sidebar:
  order: 20
---
**Set your usual payment terms once, such as 7 days or 30 days. Every new invoice then gets its due date filled in from its issue date.**

### **Set the Terms**

1. Go to **Settings → Invoice Settings**.
1. Open the **General** tab.
1. Scroll down to **Default payment terms**.
1. In **Days until due**, type a number of days from `0` to `365`. Or click a quick choice:
   - **On receipt** – due on the issue date (`0` days)
   - **1 days**, **3 days**, **7 days**, **10 days**, **14 days**, **30 days**
   - **No default** – turns the default off
1. Click **Save Changes** in that section.

Leave the field empty if you do not want a default.

### **What Happens on a New Invoice**

The due date is the issue date plus your number of days.

| Issue date | Days until due | Due date |
|---|---|---|
| 25 Nov 2026 | 7 | 2 Dec 2026 |
| 25 Nov 2026 | 30 | 25 Dec 2026 |
| 25 Nov 2026 | 0 (On receipt) | 25 Nov 2026 |

When you create an invoice, the **Due Date** field is already filled in.

- If you change the **Issue Date**, the due date moves with it.
- Once you pick a **Due Date** yourself, Taskip stops changing it.
- You can always change the due date later on the invoice.

### **Invoices Created Any Other Way**

The default also fills in the due date of an invoice that has no due date, however it was created. That includes invoices made from a quotation, from billable hours, from a workflow, or through the API.

An invoice that already has a due date keeps it.

{% table %}
---
- **Note:** The setting applies to invoices only. Quotations do not use it. Invoices you created before you set the terms are not changed.
{% /table %}
